# CEO's OS — Golden Dataset Rule
Critical calculations must be anchored to Golden Datasets.
Default location:
docs/testing/golden_inputs.json
A calculation without a Golden Dataset is not pilot-ready.
## Applies To
For every:
- M&A valuation
- M&A waterfall
- buyer matching score
- risk score
- compliance score
- resilience score
- funding runway
- dilution
- post-money valuation
- investor readiness
- PMI synergy forecast
- PMI captured synergies
- integration health
- Bridge priority
- Bridge recalculate signal
- Risk heatmap
- Reporting KPI
- Executive Overview readiness radar
- board pack metric
you must:
1. Check whether a Golden Dataset exists.
2. Compare actual frontend output to expected output.
3. Compare actual backend output to expected output.
4. Verify tolerance.
5. Document mismatch.
6. Stop if mismatch affects business truthfulness.
7. Mark missing Golden Dataset as P1 before pilot readiness.
## Test Validity
A test is not sufficient if it only checks:
- render
- smoke
- happy path
- no crash
- no NaN
- snapshot
A valid calculation test must check expected business output.
## Oracle Protection
If code output mismatches a Golden Dataset, do not modify the Golden Dataset, test expectations, fixtures, tolerances or snapshots to make the code pass.
First fix business logic.
Only modify a Golden Dataset/test oracle in a separate explicit task with:
- manual calculation
- business justification
- reviewer note
- before/after expected values
## Required Table
When auditing calculations, produce this table:
| Calculation | Golden Dataset ID | Inputs | Expected | Actual frontend | Actual backend | Match | Risk | Action |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
## Stop Condition
If actual output differs from expected output beyond tolerance, stop and report:
- calculation affected
- file/function responsible
- input used
- output obtained
- expected output
- difference
- possible cause
- business risk
- recommended fix
Do not continue and do not mark the phase as closed until the mismatch is resolved or formally documented.
